# Ask Claude Code

Run exactly one command. Do not add pipes, redirects, `;` or `&&`: the
permission rule then stops matching, and a headless run denies the call.

To ask a question (Claude reads and searches, and changes nothing):

    @ASK_CLAUDE@ --read-only --dir <absolute project folder> '<the task>'

To have Claude change files:

    @ASK_CLAUDE@ --allow-write --dir <absolute project folder> '<the task>'

## Rules

- Put `--read-only` or `--allow-write` first, then the other flags, then the
  task as one argument in single quotes. Inside the quotes, write a single
  quote as two single quotes in PowerShell, and as `'\''` in sh. Avoid double
  quotes in the task: Windows PowerShell 5.1 drops them on the way.
- Use `--allow-write` only when the user asked for a change. Claude then edits
  files under `--dir` but cannot run commands, so run the build and the tests
  yourself afterwards and check `git diff`.
- `--model opus` (the default) for reasoning, `sonnet` for speed, `haiku` for
  cheap triage. `--effort low|medium|high|xhigh|max`; the default is `high`.
- Claude starts with no memory of this conversation. Put everything it needs
  into the task. Name the files it should read; do not paste their contents.
  Ask for a bounded answer, for example "at most 15 lines" or "a list of
  path:line".
- The answer arrives on stdout. One line on stderr gives the model, the time
  and the cost. Relay the answer as it is, and open the path:line references
  it cites before you act on them.
- An error about credentials or OAuth means the user has to run `claude` once
  in a terminal to sign in again. Report that and stop.
